{"id":"subordinate-traffic-attributed-backwards","title":"Subordinate assignments and reports are attributed to each other's sender","prompt":"Two tests in the core suite fail: `subordinate event admission (EventLog round-trip) > a published task admits, pends, and drains with the workspace source line` and `subordinate event admission (EventLog round-trip) > a published report admits and drains with the subordinate source line`. Delegated work is rendered with the wrong origin — an incoming assignment reads as if a subordinate sent it, and a subordinate's report reads as if it came from the orchestrating workspace. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/events/hub/visibility.ts"],"tags":["events-hub","visibility","guard"]}
{"id":"run-event-indices-start-off","title":"A fresh run's first event index is wrong, shifting every replay window","prompt":"Seven tests in the core suite fail, including `RunEventRecorder.emit > assigns monotonic eventIndex starting at 0` and `RunEventRecorder.read > honors since lower bound`. The indices handed out for a run that has no events yet do not begin where every reader assumes, so pagination bounds and SSE replay windows over the durable event log are all displaced. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/events/recorder.ts"],"tags":["run-events","indexing","boundary"]}
{"id":"zombie-executor-can-settle-job","title":"A reclaimed background job can still be completed by the fenced-off executor","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `BackgroundJobStore > lease-epoch fencing: a stale-epoch completion write is rejected, monotonic`. After an eviction reclaims a background job under a new lease, the executor left over from the dead process can still write a completion, so a zombie result lands on a job that another activation now owns. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/jobs/store.ts"],"tags":["background-jobs","fencing","guard"]}
{"id":"cancel-spares-running-jobs","title":"Operator cancel refuses in-flight jobs and acts on settled ones","prompt":"Two tests in the core suite fail: `BackgroundJobRunner.cancel — operator hard-cancel > cancel aborts the work + marks cancelled; the fiber does not relabel or wake` and `BackgroundJobRunner.cancel — operator hard-cancel > cancelRunning aborts every running job and leaves settled jobs alone`. A hard cancel now hits exactly the wrong set of jobs: work that is still in flight keeps running and is never aborted, while jobs that already finished are the ones the control plane tries to stop. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/jobs/runner.ts"],"tags":["background-jobs","lifecycle","guard"]}
{"id":"branch-takes-credited-to-wrong-side","title":"A settled steer-branch credits each answer to the other origin","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `settleBranchIntoTakes — honest settle into ONE takes pipeline > a completed branch + completed live turn persist a claimed branch-sourced pair`. When a mid-turn redirect settles against the finished turn, the persisted pair labels the live answer as the branch's and the branch's answer as the live one, so the comparison the user is shown — and the outcome ledger a pick writes — are inverted. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/steer-branch.ts"],"tags":["steer-branch","alternate-takes","ordering"]}
{"id":"auto-titling-skips-placeholder-workspaces","title":"Workspaces still showing their raw slug are never auto-titled","prompt":"Seven tests in the core suite fail, including `automatic workspace titling — the decision > a workspace still showing its raw slug is titled from its mission` and `automatic workspace titling — the decision > a stated persona wins over the opening sentence`. The titling decision now declines the very workspaces it exists to heal, so one that has never been given a name keeps displaying its raw identifier forever. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/identity/naming.ts"],"tags":["identity","naming","guard"]}
{"id":"boilerplate-mission-treated-as-real","title":"Seeded boilerplate missions are no longer recognised as placeholders","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `automatic workspace titling — the decision > no mission to title from is a no-op`. The generic mission text seeded into workspaces created without one is treated as a real, workspace-specific mission, so the agent happily derives a name and summary from boilerplate that only describes the runtime itself. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/identity/soul.ts"],"tags":["identity","soul","default"]}
{"id":"fork-drops-the-cut-point-message","title":"A fork loses the message it was cut at","prompt":"Three tests in the core suite fail, including `forkWorkspaceStorage > 9. forkPointMs is inclusive (messages at created_at == T are copied)` and `forkWorkspaceStorage > 1. preserves messages 0..N with identical PKs and parent_ids`. Forking a workspace at a chosen message produces a child whose copied history stops short of that message, so the conversation the fork inherits is one turn thinner than the point it was cut at. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/identity/fork.ts"],"tags":["identity","fork","boundary"]}
{"id":"anthropic-cache-hits-unreported","title":"Cached-prefix tokens from the Anthropic path never reach the step event","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `ChatEvent cached-token fidelity > step-finish carries usage.cachedInputTokens plus the Anthropic providerMetadata read`. Prompt-cache telemetry under-counts on the Anthropic path: the per-step event carries only what the flat usage field reports, so tokens actually served from the provider's cached prefix are invisible to the accumulator. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/chat.ts"],"tags":["chat","cache-telemetry","default"]}
{"id":"clamp-tail-not-anchored","title":"Clamped tool results blow past the character budget","prompt":"Six tests in the core suite fail, including `clampToolResult > 500KB output → clamped head+tail; the full output round-trips via the VFS path in the marker` and `clampToolResult > honours a custom budget`. Oversize tool output is no longer reduced to a bounded excerpt — the \"truncated\" result it hands back still runs far past the configured character budget, so one big command output can still rot the session. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/tools/clamp.ts"],"tags":["tools","boundary","arithmetic"]}
{"id":"clamp-restore-path-unreadable","title":"Truncation marker advertises a restore path the file surface cannot read","prompt":"Two tests in the core suite fail: `clampToolResult > 500KB output → clamped head+tail; the full output round-trips via the VFS path in the marker` and `web_search / web_fetch builtins > web_fetch clamps a big page to a head with a VFS restore path`. When a big tool result is offloaded to the workspace, the location the truncation marker hands back to the model is not in the form the workspace file surface accepts, so the omitted output is no longer recoverable. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/tools/clamp.ts"],"tags":["tools","vfs","default"]}
{"id":"head-child-outlives-parent-clock","title":"A spawned sub-head gets a deadline later than its parent's","prompt":"Three tests in the core suite fail, including `deriveChildBudget — recursive wall-clock bounded by parent remaining > a child cannot outlive the parent's remaining wall-clock even at ratio 1` and `deriveChildBudget — recursive wall-clock bounded by parent remaining > a 3-deep recursive split keeps every descendant under the operator ceiling`. A head spawned from a parent is handed a wall-clock deadline that falls after the parent's own, so a recursive split multiplies the operator's ceiling on every level it descends. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/heads/types.ts"],"tags":["heads","budget","boundary"]}
{"id":"head-token-ceiling-off-by-one","title":"A head that spent exactly its token allowance still counts as having budget","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `budgetExhausted — token ceiling gates on consumed tokens > at/over the ceiling: exhausted with reason tokens`. A head that has spent precisely its whole token allowance is still reported as having budget left, so the ceiling only bites once it has already been overrun. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/heads/types.ts"],"tags":["heads","budget","boundary"]}
{"id":"merge-ensemble-picks-outlier","title":"The k-sample merge keeps the most extreme synthesis instead of the median one","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `k-sample median merge > grounded merge runs k samples and keeps the median-scored one`. When several independent merge syntheses are drawn and scored, the one furthest from the middle of the pack is the one kept, so the merge reports whichever candidate the judge rated most extremely rather than the representative answer. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/heads/controller.ts"],"tags":["heads","ensemble","ordering"]}
{"id":"split-cost-summary-undercounts","title":"A split's cost summary undercounts what its heads spent","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `HeadController.run > spawns all heads, records journal entries, returns merged narrative`. The cost summary a completed split reports back understates the tokens its heads actually consumed, so budget accounting and the surfaced spend are both wrong. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/heads/controller.ts"],"tags":["heads","cost","field"]}
{"id":"failed-head-not-floored","title":"A head that never finished is scored as if it produced a usable result","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `grounded head outcome scores > a non-completed head is floored below a completed one without a judge call`. A head that was aborted or blew its budget is given the same standing as one whose work actually ran and held up, so a dead thread can tie with a real finding when the merge weighs them. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/heads/controller.ts"],"tags":["heads","scoring","degenerate"]}
{"id":"head-summary-takes-earliest-text","title":"A tool-final head reports its first remark as its answer","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `extractFinalText > recovers the LAST text-bearing step when result.text is empty (head ended on a tool call)`. When a head finishes on a tool call rather than prose, the summary lifted from its run is an early intermediate remark instead of the conclusion it actually settled on — so the merge synthesizes over stale text. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/heads/head-summary.ts"],"tags":["heads","ordering","summary"]}
{"id":"head-loop-ignores-token-gate","title":"A head keeps reasoning after its token budget is gone","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `runHeadInference — token budget actually stops a run > a run that overspends its token budget stops with budget_exceeded`. A head's reasoning loop carries on once it has burned through its token allowance and only halts when it runs into the step cap, so the token ceiling no longer bounds a run mid-flight. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/heads/head-inference.ts"],"tags":["heads","budget","guard"]}
{"id":"head-run-renders-without-heads","title":"A recorded split renders with none of its heads","prompt":"Two tests in the core suite fail: `HeadJournal.listRuns — grouping (the #179 quirk fix) > a top-level split (synthetic root, all heads parent_id NULL) is ONE run, not N` and `HeadController.run > records the per-head step trace; listRuns round-trips it`. A journalled split comes back with an empty roster of heads, so none of the per-head summaries or step traces reach the reasoning surface. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/heads/journal.ts"],"tags":["heads","journal","guard"]}
{"id":"approval-picks-milder-verdict","title":"A command tripping two safety rules settles on the milder verdict","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `reviewCommand > picks the highest-severity decision when multiple rules fire`. When a shell command trips more than one safety rule, the review settles on the less serious of the outcomes, so a privileged operation can come back merely flagged as unusual and execute without asking. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/safety/approval-gate.ts"],"tags":["safety","ordering","default"]}
{"id":"argument-digest-key-order-unstable","title":"Structurally identical arguments hash to different digests","prompt":"Three tests in the core suite fail, including `argumentDigest > is deterministic and order-independent over object keys` and `executionPathSignals — redundancy > argument key order does not change a call's identity`. Two argument payloads that are structurally the same now produce different digests depending on the order their keys happened to be inserted, so the commitment an approval makes to an invocation no longer survives a round trip. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/safety/argument-digest.ts"],"tags":["safety","determinism","ordering"]}
{"id":"extension-tools-always-collide","title":"Every extension-contributed tool is rejected as a name collision","prompt":"Three tests in the core suite fail, including `ExtensionHost > tools() throws on a name collision so one plugin cannot shadow another` and `extension seam through runChat > hooks fire in order around a turn, and registerTools reaches the model`. Contributing a tool from an extension now raises a collision error for a name nothing else has claimed, while a genuine duplicate would slip through — so no plugin tool reaches the turn at all. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/extension.ts"],"tags":["extensions","guard","tools"]}
{"id":"eval-budget-clamp-collapses","title":"Every operator-set eval budget collapses to the same value","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `AgentConfigStore — GEPA eval budget > defaults to 24, reads stored values, clamps to 4..64, ignores garbage`. An operator-chosen evaluation budget that sits comfortably inside the permitted range is not honoured — whatever they store, the same number comes back out. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/config/store.ts"],"tags":["config","boundary","default"]}
{"id":"think-caller-tuning-clobbered","title":"Injected defaults override the caller's per-strategy tuning","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `createThinkTool — strategy dispatch > deep-merges caller options under injected infra (host deps survive)`. Per-strategy tuning supplied on the tool call is silently overridden by the host's injected defaults instead of sitting on top of them, so a caller can no longer change any knob the host also sets. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/strategy/think-tool.ts"],"tags":["strategy","merge","ordering"]}
{"id":"peer-ask-timeout-pinned-to-ceiling","title":"Every peer ask waits the maximum timeout regardless of what was requested","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `peers tool — actions (the moved cross-workspace capability) > ask defaults: topic \"message\", timeout 120s; clamp to [5s, 600s]`. A caller-supplied wait for a peer's reply is not held inside the allowed range — every request, and the default, ends up pinned to the longest permitted wait. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/tools/builtins.ts"],"tags":["tools","peers","boundary"]}
{"id":"url-safety-private-ip-inverted","title":"The SSRF pre-flight check lets private addresses through and blocks public ones","prompt":"Two tests in the core suite fail: `url safety (SSRF + exfil guards) > blocks private / metadata / non-http targets` and `url safety (SSRF + exfil guards) > provider.fetch refuses an unsafe URL`. The pre-flight guard over outbound web fetches has its verdict backwards for raw IP targets — cloud-metadata and RFC1918 addresses are now reachable from the agent, while ordinary public ones are refused. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/web/url-safety.ts"],"tags":["url-safety","guard","security"]}
{"id":"url-safety-secret-prefix-too-long","title":"A short API key embedded in a URL is no longer recognised as a secret","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `url safety (SSRF + exfil guards) > blocks URLs carrying an embedded secret`. The exfiltration guard only recognises a credential smuggled into a fetch target when it is unusually long, so shorter but perfectly real keys travel out inside the URL. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/web/url-safety.ts"],"tags":["url-safety","boundary","security"]}
{"id":"device-mount-consent-prefix-escape","title":"A sibling directory sharing the consented prefix escapes the /pc consent scope","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `device mount adapter (raw DeviceTransport + consent scope) > SECURITY: escaping the consented subtree is denied without the full-fs tier`. The path-scope check on the user's device mount compares against the consented directory by text alone, so a sibling directory whose name merely begins with the consented one is treated as living inside it. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/vfs/mount-adapters.ts"],"tags":["vfs-mounts","boundary","security"]}
{"id":"stat-line-size-and-mtime-swapped","title":"Synthesized stat reports the modification time as the file size and vice versa","prompt":"Four tests in the core suite fail, including `nimbus mount adapter (raw NimbusSandboxHandle) > stat is synthesized via stat(1) with real size + mtime` and `VFS conformance — nimbus mount adapter > stat of a written file reports size + isDir:false`. Mounts that have no native stat and synthesize one from a shell probe now report each file's size and its modification time as each other. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/vfs/mount-adapters.ts"],"tags":["vfs-mounts","ordering","arithmetic"]}
{"id":"composite-mount-root-unguarded","title":"A mount root is treated as an ordinary file instead of a directory","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `Phase 1 — synthetic read-only '/' > mount roots: writable-looking ops are guarded, reads delegate`. The workspace file plane no longer recognises when an addressed path IS a mount root, so operations that should be refused because the target is a directory are instead passed down into the environment. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/vfs/composite.ts"],"tags":["vfs-mounts","guard","degenerate"]}
{"id":"composite-root-lists-dead-mounts","title":"The mount table lists environments that are not currently available","prompt":"Two tests in the core suite fail: `Phase 1 — synthetic read-only '/' > readdir('/') lists live mount names only` and `composite routing over the mount adapters > readdir('/') tracks the /pc mount's live device connection`. Listing the workspace mount table now advertises environments that are not reachable at that moment, so a disconnected device still appears alongside the ones the agent can actually use. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/vfs/composite.ts"],"tags":["vfs-mounts","guard","default"]}
{"id":"product-path-escape-guard-dropped","title":"A product path climbing above the source root is reported as the wrong failure","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `product-change path safety > rejects outside-root and secret paths`. Normalizing a product-change path no longer notices when the path walks up past the source root, so traversal out of the repository is not called out for what it is. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/product-change/path-safety.ts"],"tags":["path-safety","boundary","guard"]}
{"id":"deploy-digest-null-patch-conflated","title":"A deploy approval binds an absent patch and an empty patch to the same digest","prompt":"One test in the core suite fails: `deployApprovalDigest > null command and null patch are bound distinctly (not conflated)`. The commitment an approval makes to the artifact it authorizes cannot tell \"there was no patch\" apart from \"the patch was empty\", so those two materially different deploys share one approval. Fix the source so the entire `bun test --cwd packages/core` suite passes and `tsc --noEmit -p packages/core` is clean. Do not modify any test file.","suite":"core","editable":["packages/core/src/product-change/approval-digest.ts"],"tags":["approvals","degenerate","security"]}
